1. My setup:

Windows Laptop
     |
    WSL
     |
     +------ EC2-1
     |
     +------ EC2-2

Assume:

EC2-1 = 1.2.3.4
EC2-2 = 5.6.7.8

Use your actual public IPs.

Step 1: Create lab directory

Inside WSL:

mkdir ~/ansible-ec2-lab
cd ~/ansible-lab

Step 2: Create inventory
nano inventory

Add:

[web]
web1 ansible_host=1.2.3.4
web2 ansible_host=5.6.7.8


[all:vars]
ansible_user=ubuntu

Replace the IPs with your EC2 public IPs.

Step 3: Configure SSH key

If your EC2 key is in your WSL home directory:

chmod 400 ~/my-key.pem

Then add this to inventory:

[web]
web1 ansible_host=1.2.3.4 ansible_ssh_private_key_file=~/my-key.pem
web2 ansible_host=5.6.7.8 ansible_ssh_private_key_file=~/my-key.pem


[all:vars]
ansible_user=ubuntu
cd ~/ansible-ec2-la

Step 4: Create ansible.cfg
nano ansible.cfg

Put:

[defaults]
inventory = ./inventory
host_key_checking = False

Now Ansible knows:

Whenever I run Ansible from this directory, use ./inventory as my inventory.

Step 5: Test

Normally you would do:

ansible all -i inventory -m ping

But because ansible.cfg already specifies the inventory, you can simply do:

ansible all -m ping

Expected:

web1 | SUCCESS => {
    "changed": false,
    "ping": "pong"
}

web2 | SUCCESS => {
    "changed": false,
    "ping": "pong"
}

Note:- Before eunning ansible command, ssh your EC2 instance for testing.

Suppose EC2-1 public IP is:

13.123.45.67

Run:

ssh -i ~/testing.pem ubuntu@13.123.45.67

If you get:

Are you sure you want to continue connecting (yes/no/[fingerprint])?

type:

yes

If you successfully get something like:

ubuntu@ip-172-31-xx-xx:~$

then your WSL → EC2 SSH connection is working.

Exit:

exit

Do the same for EC2-2.

2. What does this word "host_key_checking" means?

=> host_key_checking is related to SSH host verification.

When you connect to an EC2 server for the first time manually:

ssh -i ~/testing.pem ubuntu@15.252.123.99

SSH may ask:

Are you sure you want to continue connecting (yes/no/[fingerprint])?

This happens because your WSL machine has never connected to that server before.

SSH stores the server's identity/fingerprint in:

~/.ssh/known_hosts
